 "id": "06-s06-what-it-reveals",
 "text": "But there's something important to understand. Echolocation isn't like suddenly gaining a second pair of eyes. You don't see colors, read signs, or recognize someone's face through sound. And different surfaces behave very differently. Some reflect sound strongly. Others absorb much of it. So even a skilled echolocator has to interpret incomplete information. The world isn't becoming a perfect picture. It's becoming a collection of useful clues.",
 "seconds": 30.15,
